                <pb facs="0781" n="669" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg669"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12941: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-29T18:00:00-05:00">January 29, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>27. Your Nos. 37, January 26, noon, and 47, January 29, 5 p.m. The Department
                    concurs in your proposed reply to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.</p>
                <p>The Department’s position in regard to the transportation of Government troops as
                    set forth in its No. 358, October 27, 1933, 4 p.m.<anchor n="77"
                        xml:id="d653-f1"/> remains unchanged. The Department also concurs in the
                    view expressed in the last sentence of your telegram No. 47, January 29, 5 p.m.
                    and considers that the Legation should not inform American companies of the
                    contents of the Foreign Office note.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
